program            = 003_struct_and_pointer_args
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Aggregates and pointers through the variadic path, sized around the aggregate by-register versus by-memory boundary on the three register-passing targets and contrasted against i686's all-stack convention, folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Header exception: this program includes stdarg.h. The corpus sanctions two header exception
classes and only two, and this is the first of them: the programs in area 07 may include
<stdarg.h> and nothing else. The second class is the dedicated bundled-header probe
12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c, which may include the nine required bundled
freestanding headers; it does not apply here, and no program may take both. The exception is
needed because a variadic function cannot be written at all without
va_list, va_start, va_arg and va_end. stdarg.h belongs to bcc's own bundled freestanding
set (docs/technical-specifications.md line 208, the row that also lists va_copy) and is a
freestanding header the reference compiler supplies, so it resolves the same way under
both oracles and the program stays a single-file reproducer. No other header is included;
in particular stdio.h is not, because bcc ships none, so including it would manufacture a
divergence caused by the test rather than by the compiler. printf is hand-declared as
int printf(const char *, ...); and no other library function is declared at all.
Pointer type compatibility: int * and const int * are not compatible types, and va_arg
requires the retrieval type to be compatible with the type of the argument actually
supplied. sum_via_pointers retrieves with va_arg(ap_pt, const int *), so every pointer
argument is written at the call site with an explicit (const int *) cast. A bare address
of an int object would supply an int * where a const int * is retrieved, which is
undefined behaviour and would silently invalidate both differential oracles.
Aggregates are not promoted: an aggregate passed through an ellipsis undergoes no default
argument promotion, so va_arg(ap_sm, struct small), va_arg(ap_md, struct mid) and
va_arg(ap_lg, struct large) retrieve exactly the types the caller supplied.
Variadic discipline: va_start names count, the last named parameter, in all four helpers.
Each helper retrieves exactly count arguments and each call site supplies exactly that
many, so no va_list is ever read past its arguments. Each va_start has exactly one
matching va_end in the same function. No va_copy and no va_list forwarding appear here;
those belong to 004_va_copy_multiple_passes and 005_va_list_forwarding.
Object lifetime and initialization: every folded struct object names every member, and
every runtime struct object has every member assigned from volatile storage before it is
passed, so there is no read of uninitialized storage and no missing field initializer.
Every element of both volatile source arrays is written before any of them is read. Every
index into the eight-element array member stays within 0 to 7. All pointed-to objects are
locals of main whose lifetime spans the entire call, so no dangling pointer is formed.
Arithmetic: every value and every partial sum is a small integer far inside the 32-bit int
range - the largest printed is 3672 - so no signed overflow is possible. There is no
shift, no division, no aliasing violation, no object modified twice between sequence
points, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the addresses of unrelated objects.
Evaluation order: main stores each helper's return value in a named temporary before
printing it, so no call carries more than one side-effecting argument and the print order
is fixed however the arguments are evaluated. In the runtime variants the volatile source
is read into plain objects by statements that precede the call, so the arguments of each
call expression are pure reads of plain objects and the unspecified order in which they
are evaluated is unobservable.
Warning gate: this program passes the full default gate - -Wall -Wextra -pedantic
-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror - with no diagnostic, which is why no
ub_audit_flags key appears above. That key exists to declare a deviation, a deviation is
modelled as a removal from the fixed gate, and a value naming the entire gate would
deviate in nothing; omitting the key is therefore how a program that satisfies the gate in
full records that it does. Area 07 sanctions no deviation and none is needed.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Aggregate sizes, and why they are identical on every target: every member of every struct
is an int, and int is 4 bytes on all four targets - measured with each target's own
reference driver rather than assumed. sizeof(struct small) is therefore 8, sizeof(struct
mid) is 16 and sizeof(struct large) is 32 on x86-64, i686, AArch64 and RISC-V 64 alike,
which was measured on all four as well. No struct contains a pointer, a long, a double or
a bitfield: the documented target table (docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457 to
462) records a pointer and long width of 4 bytes on i686 against 8 on the other three, so
a pointer or long member would make one declaration a different size on i686 and the
retrieved values could no longer be compared across backends.
Why these three sizes, and why the claim is made per target rather than universally. They
are chosen to sit below, at, and above the aggregate by-register versus by-memory boundary
on the three targets that have such a boundary, and the reasoning leans only on the ABI
inventories the repository actually documents. x86-64 System V AMD64
(docs/technical-specifications.md line 546) classifies each argument INTEGER, SSE or MEMORY
and lists six integer argument registers together with eight SSE registers, so an aggregate
classified INTEGER travels in registers and one classified MEMORY travels on the stack.
AArch64 AAPCS64 (line 559) documents eight integer argument registers x0 to x7, and
RISC-V 64 LP64D (line 565) documents eight, a0 to a7; each therefore has a register path an
aggregate can occupy and a memory path it falls back to.
i686 has no such boundary at all, and that is part of the coverage rather than a gap in it.
cdecl (line 553) passes every argument on the stack, right to left, with caller cleanup, so
all three aggregates travel the same way on i686 whatever their size. That makes i686 the
control arm of this program: three sizes routed three different ways on the register-passing
targets are routed identically here, and the retrieved values must still agree with all
three. A divergence between i686 and the baseline consequently cannot be an aggregate
classification-threshold difference, which narrows the diagnosis considerably.
Where each of the three sizes actually lands on each register-passing target is that ABI's
decision, and this record deliberately does not predict it: the repository documents register
inventories rather than aggregate classification rules, and the suite is a black-box oracle
that compares only the retrieved values. A correct backend agrees with the other three
however it routes the arguments.
No addresses printed: the pointer group prints only the dereferenced value. No address, no
pointer value, no sizeof result and no signedness-dependent plain char value reaches
stdout, so nothing width-dependent or signedness-dependent can enter the golden record. The
only printf conversions used are %s and %d, and every %s argument is an ASCII string
literal whose bytes are identical on every target.
Nothing here narrows coverage: all four targets, all three optimization levels and all
three oracles are declared, and no expected-divergence marker applies, area 07 having
none. These notes therefore record why the comparison is sound rather than an exclusion
from it. A cross-backend difference in this program is a genuine ABI-level finding rather
than a documented implementation-defined difference, which is exactly why the
cross-backend oracle is enabled here.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
